 ![](images/eb/eb3981cb149c9d1b26c2b87bd92a5aef5ead1787d8cb817fa3c1efd6a66074f9.svg) and ![](images/e9/e988f1ab98136f2d888e83baf436d8f659654a55eb2d2b3e11029b365a52c6e0.svg)" 13194139534600 17592186045417 true] [17592186124380 1071 "![](images/23/2379f9f0cec3f257da5a4c0695f114d0bc7f3b4c914edabfd690ff94705faceb.png)\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\lambda=4$ is an eigenvalue of $P$\n\n- $\\lambda=-1$ is an eigenvalue of $P$\n\n- The eigenvalue $\\lambda=6$ has algebraic multiplicity $2$" 13194139534600 17592186045417 false] [17592186124380 1071 "![](images/2e/2e17e4fa743f6f8d3f5987b9095cf6976ae8eadf36ee4456ea8a9195201526e4.svg)\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\lambda=4$ is an eigenvalue of $P$\n\nII. $\\lambda=-1$ is an eigenvalue of $P$\n\nIII. The eigenvalue $\\lambda=6$ has algebraic multiplicity $2$" 13194139534600 17592186045417 true] [17592186124380 1074 "First, we find the matrix $\\, P-\\lambda I \\mathbin{:}$\n﻿﻿﻿![](images/38/38316eff018e61b4f9f6ce90e0e231ed1eeb6fe993f3b107a5ad8057e3641fd4.svg)\n\nThe characteristic equation is $\\det(P-\\lambda I) = 0.$ \n\nNotice that we can expand the $4 \\times 4$ determinant across the $1$st column, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & 0 & -8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1-\\lambda  & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 4-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 7 & -3 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, expanding the $3 \\times 3$ determinant across the $2$nd row, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3-\\lambda )\\cdot (4-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ 7 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )((-1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-35) & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-36) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)(\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -6)(\\lambda +6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $P$ are $\\lambda=3,$ $\\lambda=4,$ $\\lambda=6,$ and $\\lambda=-6$ (all with algebraic multiplicity $1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534600 17592186045417 true] [17592186124380 1074 "First, we find the matrix $\\, P-\\lambda I \\mathbin{:}$\n﻿﻿﻿![](images/8e/8ee9ef991440e5641d6a458c0384382b54eb3d8504856b942fc33cf24b0b85e9.png)\n\nThe characteristic equation is $\\det(P-\\lambda I) = 0.$ \n\nNotice that we can expand the $4 \\times 4$ determinant across the $1$st column, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & 0 & -8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1-\\lambda  & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 4-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 7 & -3 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, expanding the $3 \\times 3$ determinant across the $2$nd row, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3-\\lambda )\\cdot (4-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ 7 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )((-1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-35) & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-36) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)(\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -6)(\\lambda +6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $P$ are $\\lambda=3,$ $\\lambda=4,$ $\\lambda=6,$ and $\\lambda=-6$ (all with algebraic multiplicity $1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534600 17592186045417 false] [17592186124522 1038 #edb/ref 17592186427385 13194139534600 17592186045417 true] [17592186124749 1050 1080.0 13194139534600 17592186045417 true] [17592186125013 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534600 17592186045417 true] [17592186125013 1071 "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139534600 17592186045417 false] [17592186125013 1071 "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -5\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139534600 17592186045417 true] [17592186125013 1074 "First, we find the matrix $\\,A-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}-\\lambda  & -2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3-\\lambda  & 6 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $det(A-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nExpanding the determinant across the $1$st row, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-\\lambda  & -2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3-\\lambda  & 6 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ -\\lambda |\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & 6 \\\\ -2 & -5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}|-(-2)|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}|+(-3)|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3-\\lambda  \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ -\\lambda ({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -3)+2(2\\lambda -2)-3(2\\lambda -2) & =0 \\\\ (-{\\lambda }^{3}-2{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda )+(4\\lambda -4)-(6\\lambda -6) & =0 \\\\ -{\\lambda }^{3}-2{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda +2 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534600 17592186045417 true] [17592186125785 1064 "We can find the eigenvalues of a $3\\times 3$ matrix in much the same way as for $2\\!\\times\\! 2$ matrices. \n\nRemember, $\\lambda$ is an eigenvalue of $A$ if $A\\mathbf{v} = \\lambda\\mathbf{v}$ for some non-zero vector $\\mathbf{v}$. This implies that the equation\n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nhas a non-trivial solution, which means that the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ is singular, and consequently\n$\\textrm{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0.$\nTherefore, if we want to find the eigenvalues of a $n\\!\\times\\! n$ matrix $A,$ we have to find the solutions of the characteristic equation\n\n$$\n\\textrm{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0.\n$$\n\nLet's go through this process in the case of the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n![](images/21/21e0994519fbba32a84020ce4661e98600c1307947083ddc3afe9902658143c1.svg)\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix $A-\\lambda I{:}$\n\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\lambda \\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set and solve the characteristic equation $\\,\\textrm{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0.$ Using the fact that the determinant of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix is given by the product of the elements on the main diagonal, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-2-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )(-8-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda +8)(\\lambda -5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are $\\lambda=-2,$ $\\lambda=-8,$ and $\\lambda=5.$" 13194139534600 17592186045417 true] [17592186125785 1064 "We can find the eigenvalues of a $3\\times 3$ matrix in much the same way as for $2\\!\\times\\! 2$ matrices. \n\nRemember, $\\lambda$ is an eigenvalue of $A$ if $A\\mathbf{v} = \\lambda\\mathbf{v}$ for some non-zero vector $\\mathbf{v}$. This implies that the equation\n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nhas a non-trivial solution, which means that the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ is singular, and consequently\n$\\textrm{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0.$\nTherefore, if we want to find the eigenvalues of a $n\\!\\times\\! n$ matrix $A,$ we have to find the solutions of the characteristic equation\n\n$$\n\\textrm{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0.\n$$\n\nLet's go through this process in the case of the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix $A-\\lambda I{:}$\n\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\lambda \\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set and solve the characteristic equation $\\,\\textrm{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0.$ Using the fact that the determinant of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix is given by the product of the elements on the main diagonal, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-2-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )(-8-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda +8)(\\lambda -5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are $\\lambda=-2,$ $\\lambda=-8,$ and $\\lambda=5.$" 13194139534600 17592186045417 false] [17592186366006 1071 "$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -7 & 6 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $C$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $C$\n- $\\lambda =5$ is an eigenvalue of $C$\n- The eigenvalue $\\lambda =5$ has algebraic multiplicity $2$" 13194139534600 17592186045417 false] [17592186366006 1071 "$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -7 & 6 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $C$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $C$\n\nII. $\\lambda =5$ is an eigenvalue of $C$\n\nIII. The eigenvalue $\\lambda =5$ has algebraic multiplicity $2$" 13194139534600 17592186045417 true] [17592186366365 1071 "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & -8 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -14\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $P$\n- $\\lambda =-14$ is an eigenvalue of $P$\n- The eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ has algebraic multiplicity $2$" 13194139534600 17592186045417 false] [17592186366365 1071 "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & -8 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -14\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $P$\n\nII. $\\lambda =-14$ is an eigenvalue of $P$\n\nIII. The eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ has algebraic multiplicity $2$" 13194139534600 17592186045417 true] [17592186427374 1080 "\\lambda \\in \\{-1,4,1\\}" 13194139534600 17592186045417 true] [17592186427374 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534600 17592186045417 true] [17592186427374 1083 #uuid "0fde5085-a635-5696-9cd7-100b3c72cad2" 13194139534600 17592186045417 true] [17592186427375 1080 "5{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -4=0" 13194139534600 17592186045417 true] [17592186427375 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534600 17592186045417 true] [17592186427375 1083 #uuid "2dc50313-1349-57e5-bd5b-c32ef21846c4" 13194139534600 17592186045417 true] [17592186427376 1080 "\\lambda \\in \\{-1,-4,1\\}" 13194139534600 17592186045417 true] [17592186427376 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534600 17592186045417 true] [17592186427376 1083 #uuid "578c36b5-c09b-5821-8e36-ba81ab9528bc" 13194139534600 17592186045417 true] [17592186427377 1080 "\\lambda \\in \\{2,0,1\\}" 13194139534600 17592186045417 true] [17592186427377 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534600 17592186045417 true] [17592186427377 1083 #uuid "78e32da7-f1e3-50b7-a080-2498d2b37830" 13194139534600 17592186045417 true] [17592186427378 1080 "{\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -2=0" 13194139534600 17592186045417 true] [17592186427378 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534600 17592186045417 true] [17592186427378 1083 #uuid "ba3bf449-9417-55d8-bda3-bcdf2a3abb0f" 13194139534600 17592186045417 true] [17592186427379 1080 "{\\lambda }^{3}-5{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda =0" 13194139534600 17592186045417 true] [17592186427379 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534600 17592186045417 true] [17592186427379 1083 #uuid "bb4847f7-518c-5520-9f7b-8f055c77b9d8" 13194139534600 17592186045417 true] [17592186427380 1080 "{\\lambda }^{3}-5{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -2=0" 13194139534600 17592186045417 true] [17592186427380 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534600 17592186045417 true] [17592186427380 1083 #uuid "c9d9c2bc-05e4-5243-ab8a-17562b551bd5" 13194139534600 17592186045417 true] [17592186427381 1080 "\\lambda \\in \\{2,4,0\\}" 13194139534600 17592186045417 true] [17592186427381 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534600 17592186045417 true] [17592186427381 1083 #uuid "cfdc823e-90d7-5645-a8fc-5def08fa694a" 13194139534600 17592186045417 true] [17592186427382 1080 "\\lambda \\in \\{-1,2,0\\}" 13194139534600 17592186045417 true] [17592186427382 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534600 17592186045417 true] [17592186427382 1083 #uuid "ed7b9e7c-c2fe-5559-be97-bb8125d6fa51" 13194139534600 17592186045417 true] [17592186427383 1080 "{\\lambda }^{3}-2\\lambda -2=0" 13194139534600 17592186045417 true] [17592186427383 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534600 17592186045417 true] [17592186427383 1083 #uuid "f431214a-fe2c-58fb-acc6-9d70412cfbb8" 13194139534600 17592186045417 true] [17592186427384 1075 #edb/ref 17592186427374 13194139534600 17592186045417 true] [17592186427384 1075 #edb/ref 17592186427376 13194139534600 17592186045417 true] [17592186427384 1075 #edb/ref 17592186427377 13194139534600 17592186045417 true] [17592186427384 1075 #edb/ref 17592186427381 13194139534600 17592186045417 true] [17592186427384 1075 #edb/ref 17592186427382 13194139534600 17592186045417 true] [17592186427384 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534600 17592186045417 true] [17592186427384 1077 "selection" 13194139534600 17592186045417 true] [17592186427384 1078 #edb/ref 17592186427374 13194139534600 17592186045417 true] [17592186427384 1079 #uuid "0da5b7ce-3185-5f7f-9a0f-797454d122c3" 13194139534600 17592186045417 true] [17592186427385 1068 #edb/ref 17592186427384 13194139534600 17592186045417 true] [17592186427385 1069 "q-36831" 13194139534600 17592186045417 true] [17592186427385 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534600 17592186045417 true] [17592186427385 1071 "Determine all real eigenvalues $\\lambda $ of the matrix \n$B=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139534600 17592186045417 true] [17592186427385 1072 #uuid "2ffb7e93-f33c-5adf-acf0-f55e6fbbb059" 13194139534600 17592186045417 true] [17592186427385 1074 "First, we find the matrix $\\,B-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nB-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $det(B-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nSince the determinant of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix is given by the product of the entries on the main diagonal, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,$ $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=1.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvalues of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix are always the entries on the main diagonal." 13194139534600 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-0fde5085-a635-5696-9cd7-100b3c72cad2" 17592186427374 "answer-2dc50313-1349-57e5-bd5b-c32ef21846c4" 17592186427375 "answer-578c36b5-c09b-5821-8e36-ba81ab9528bc" 17592186427376 "answer-78e32da7-f1e3-50b7-a080-2498d2b37830" 17592186427377 "answer-ba3bf449-9417-55d8-bda3-bcdf2a3abb0f" 17592186427378 "answer-bb4847f7-518c